{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The fixed-parameter qualification is on the wrong side of the comparison. The passage leaves finite-horizon unfolding unrefuted when the recurrent system's parameters are fixed. Lean allows P's parameters to change and still requires a matching fixed-rule N. This is a rival hypothesis to the claimed breakdown under plasticity, not the equivalence the authors say they do not refute."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Equality on histories is not the same test as change of a parameter-dependent input–output map over time. Lean runs both systems from their initial states on each history and compares their final outputs. A fixed-rule N can use its state to track the effects of P's parameter changes, so history-wise agreement is compatible with P having different input–output maps before and after a change."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "S2 does not require a recurrent neural architecture or nontrivial plasticity. Its first component can remain constant or change without affecting the dynamics of the second component. The quantifier thus ranges over arbitrary deterministic two-component systems, without identifying the rapid, function-altering plasticity at issue in the passage."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The passage gives a time horizon, not a bound of exactly T units independent of the original system's size. Lean requires that exact count but allows each unit an arbitrary state type and update function: a single unit can represent the original system's entire state and parameters. An acyclic simulator with that count need not be a unit-by-unit unfolding of the recurrent network."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The target poses a finite-history simulation question for adaptive systems. It neither stays within the fixed-parameter scope of the unrefuted result nor tests the time-dependent loss of equivalence described in the passage."
}
